Based on the details you provided for your 2023 taxes, here is a breakdown of how your situation is generally treated, what you can deduct, and the forms you will need.

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should take the Standard Deduction.** 
For the 2023 tax year, the standard deduction for a Single filer is **$13,850**. 
To itemize, your eligible expenses (like charitable donations, state/local taxes, and mortgage interest) would need to exceed $13,850. Your known itemizable expenses are your $1,200 donation to Second Harvest Heartland and your Colorado state income taxes (which would be roughly $3,000-$3,500). Since this total is far below $13,850, taking the standard deduction is much more beneficial for you. 

*Note: Because you are taking the standard deduction, your $1,200 charitable donation will not provide an additional tax benefit for 2023 (the special pandemic-era above-the-line deduction for charity expired).*

### 2. Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($420)
* **If they were for your W-2 job:** You **cannot** deduct them. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (TCJA) eliminated the deduction for unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 workers through 2025.
* **If they were for your 1099-NEC freelance work:** You **can** deduct them as a business expense against your freelance income on Schedule C.

### 3. Student Loan Interest
You paid $2,900, but the IRS caps the student loan interest deduction at **$2,500** per year. This is an "above-the-line" deduction, meaning you can take it even if you take the standard deduction. Note that this deduction phases out depending on your Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI), but based on your rough income (~$83k), you should be eligible for at least a partial, if not full, deduction.

### 4. Home Office Deduction
Because you are a W-2 employee *and* a freelancer, you can only claim the home office deduction against your **freelance (1099-NEC) income**, and the space must be used *exclusively and regularly* for your freelance business. Since you use the 180 sq ft room only as an office, it qualifies. 

You have two ways to calculate this:
* **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot up to 300 sq ft. (180 sq ft × $5 = **$900 deduction**). This is very easy and requires no record-keeping of your rent or utilities.
* **Actual Expenses Method:** You calculate the percentage of your home used for business (180 sq ft / 920 sq ft = **19.56%**). You can deduct 19.56% of your rent ($26,400/year × 19.56% = **$5,163**), plus 19.56% of your renter's insurance, utilities, and internet. 
* **Verdict:** The Actual Expenses method will give you a much larger deduction, significantly lowering your taxable freelance income.

### 5. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, your return will consist of the following forms:

* **Form 1040:** The main tax return document where your W-2 wages ($71,200) and standard deduction will be reported.
* **Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income):** Used to report your freelance business income, your student loan interest deduction, and the deductible portion of your self-employment tax.
* **Schedule 2 (Additional Taxes):** Used to calculate your Self-Employment tax on your freelance income.
* **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Because your interest income ($1,880) is over the $1,500 threshold, the IRS requires you to list the payer (American Express) on Part I of this schedule. You will also list your Vanguard dividends ($510) on Part II.
* **Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business):** This is where you will report your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your freelance expenses (like the $420 in supplies, if applicable).
* **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** Used to calculate the Social Security and Medicare taxes you owe on your freelance profit.
* **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** You will need this form if you choose the "Actual Expenses" method for your home office deduction to calculate the 19.56% of your rent and utilities. (If you use the Simplified Method, you just report it directly on Schedule C).

