Based on the details you provided for the 2023 tax year, here is a breakdown of how your situation is generally treated, what you can deduct, and the forms you will need:

### 1. Standard Deduction vs. Itemizing
**You should almost certainly take the Standard Deduction.**
*   **Standard Deduction:** For a single filer in 2023, the standard deduction is **$13,850**.
*   **Itemized Deductions (Schedule A):** Your itemizable expenses include your $1,200 charitable donation to Second Harvest Heartland and your State and Local Taxes (SALT). Even if your Colorado state income taxes are around $3,500–$4,000, your total itemized deductions would only be around $5,000–$6,000. This is well below the $13,850 standard deduction. 
*   *Note:* Because you are taking the standard deduction, the $1,200 charitable donation will not provide a tax benefit for 2023 (the special pandemic-era above-the-line deduction for charity expired).

### 2. Freelance Income & Deductions (Schedule C)
Your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income means you are considered a self-employed sole proprietor for your freelance work. You can deduct business expenses against this income to lower your taxable business profit and your self-employment tax.
*   **Unreimbursed Work Supplies ($420):** 
    *   If these supplies were for your *freelance* editing/proofreading work, you can deduct them fully on Schedule C.
    *   If these supplies were for your *W-2 job* at Greenfield Consulting, they are **not deductible**. The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act (TCJA) eliminated unreimbursed employee expenses for W-2 workers through 2025.
*   **Home Office Deduction:** Because you use the 180 sq ft room *exclusively* and *regularly* for your freelance business, you qualify for this deduction against your 1099-NEC income. You have two choices:
    *   **Simplified Method:** $5 per square foot. (180 sq ft × $5 = **$900** deduction). This is easier and requires no record-keeping of rent/utilities.
    *   **Actual Expenses Method:** Your office is 19.56% of your apartment (180 / 920 sq ft). You can deduct 19.56% of your rent ($26,400/year × 19.56% = **$5,163**), plus 19.56% of your renter's insurance, utilities, and internet. *This method will give you a much larger deduction*, significantly reducing your taxable freelance profit.

### 3. Adjustments to Income ("Above-the-Line" Deductions)
You can take these deductions even if you take the standard deduction:
*   **Student Loan Interest:** The maximum deduction is $2,500 per year (even though you paid $2,900). However, this deduction phases out for single filers in 2023 with a Modified Adjusted Gross Income (MAGI) between $75,000 and $90,000. Since your total income is roughly $83,000 (before business deductions), your $2,500 deduction will likely be partially reduced/prorated.
*   **Self-Employment Tax Deduction:** You will owe self-employment tax (Medicare and Social Security) on your net freelance profit. You get to deduct 50% of this tax amount as an adjustment to income.

### 4. Forms and Worksheets You Will Need
When you file, your return will likely include the following forms:
*   **Form 1040:** The main tax return document.
*   **Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income):** Where you will report your business income, your student loan interest deduction, and your deductible part of self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule 2 (Additional Taxes):** Where you will report your self-employment tax.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Because your interest income ($1,880) is over the $1,500 threshold, you are required to fill out Schedule B to list the payers (American Express and Vanguard).
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss From Business):** To report your $9,800 in 1099-NEC income and deduct your work supplies (if applicable) and home office expenses.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** To calculate the Social Security and Medicare taxes on your net freelance income.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** You will need this form if you choose the "Actual Expenses" method for your home office deduction (which is highly recommended in your case due to the high rent). If you use the Simplified Method, you just report it directly on Schedule C.
*   **Student Loan Interest Deduction Worksheet:** Found in the Form 1040 instructions to calculate your exact allowed deduction based on your MAGI phase-out.

